Let's run a functional test for mission 0 progression. Use eval with async function that calls game.update in loop. But we shouldn't call update while startMission's mission card is still showing? It's done already (we started earlier). The mission is active step 0.

Let's write a test eval:

```js
() => {
  const g = window.game;
  const log = [];
  const tick = (n=30) => { for (let i=0;i<n;i++) g.update(0.016); };
  // step 0: goto kiosk
  let t = g.missionManager.rawTarget();
  g.teleport(t.x + 3, t.z, Math.PI/2);
  tick(10);
  log.push({step: g.missionManager.stepIndex, type: g.missionManager.step?.type});
  // step 1 talk
  t = g.missionManager.rawTarget();
  g.teleport(t.x + 2, t.z, Math.PI/2);
  tick(5);
  log.push({dialogue: g.ui.dialogueActive, lines: g.ui.dialogueQueue.length});
  let guard=0;
  while (g.ui.dialogueActive && guard++ < 20) { g.ui.advanceDialogue(); tick(3); }
  log.push({step: g.missionManager.stepIndex, type: g.missionManager.step?.type});
  // step 2 collect car
  t = g.missionManager.rawTarget();
  g.teleport(t.x + 2, t.z, Math.PI/2);
  tick(10);
  log.push({step: ...});
  // step 3 goto marina
  ...
  // step 4 talk
  ...
  return log;
}
```

Need to be careful: missionManager.rawTarget for talk returns contact position after contact spawned on enterStep. Teleporting to t.x+2 works. For goto steps, update checks distance. But teleporting might place inside collider; doesn't matter.

Also note game.update calls updateGameplay only when playing. It will also update NPCs etc. Fine.

